A checkpoint stores enough training state to resume later.

This commonly includes model parameters, meaning the learned weights; optimizer state, the information used to calculate weight updates; progress counters; and the position in the training data.

Think of saving a document, with one extra requirement: the workers must restart from mutually consistent versions of the job.

Frequent checkpoints reduce how much work must be repeated after a failure. But writing them uses storage and network bandwidth, and can delay training. Less frequent saves reduce that routine cost but make failures more expensive.

Restart software can bring workers back after a failure. Sometimes it has to restart the whole group, as can happen with PyTorch's torchrun.

The group must load a valid saved version of its work. Getting the programs running again is only useful if they can resume training together.

4. Why one failure can be expensive

Try this: A job uses 1,024 accelerators and saves a checkpoint every 30 minutes. One worker fails 15 minutes after the last save. Repair and restart take another 10 minutes. If the whole group returns to the last save, has it lost only the failed worker's time?

No. Under these illustrative assumptions, the whole job loses 25 minutes: 15 minutes of work to repeat, plus 10 minutes to recover.

The important point is the scale of the impact. A single fault has delayed a job occupying more than a thousand accelerators. Repeated interruptions can waste a meaningful part of a very expensive system's capacity.

This is why faster recovery, more reliable links and well-chosen checkpoint intervals can be valuable even though they do not make a GPU calculate any faster.

The loss is smaller if a system can preserve more work or recover only the affected part. The example assumes the entire group must restart.



5. Fix the cause, not just the symptom

Start with a timeline: did one worker slow down, did everyone slow down, or did progress stop suddenly?

Different causes need different fixes:

- An overheated GPU may need better cooling so it can keep working at its normal speed.
- A faulty link may need a cable replaced so data stops being resent.
- Uneven work may need to be divided more fairly between workers.
- A software mismatch may need the programs to coordinate their exchanges correctly.
- A slow restart may need better checkpoint storage or recovery software.

Operators use device measurements and software logs—records of what happened—to tell these cases apart. Then they change one thing and check whether the job improves.

This matters because buying faster GPUs will not fix a faulty cable or a program that is waiting for the wrong result. Understanding the cause directs spending toward the part that actually helps.

6. Translate reliability into useful capacity

Installed accelerator count tells you how much hardware exists. It does not tell you how much lasting work the system completes.

Useful capacity also depends on power and cooling, monitoring, spare components, checkpoint storage and the people or software that restore failed jobs.

Redundancy means keeping extra capacity or alternative paths available. It costs money and sometimes power. It is valuable when the saved job time justifies that cost.

Measure completed jobs, unusually slow steps, retries, time to detect problems, time to recover and the cost of saving checkpoints. Together, these explain more than a chip count alone. For a cluster owner, the goal is more completed work from the hardware already purchased. For a supplier, the opportunity is solving a failure or delay that has a costly effect on the whole system.

Remember

- Synchronization makes healthy workers wait for required results.
- A straggler is slow; a hang is stuck; a failure removes a participant.
- Checkpoints reduce repeated work, but saving them also consumes resources.
- Recovery cost can spread across the entire job.

